{
  "dom_id": "step-q221381",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $11∣k$ then $33∣k$\n- If $33∣k$ then $11∣k$\n- If $33∣k$ then $3∣k$",
  "worked_solution": "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$ we have transitivity of divisibility:\n\n$a∣b\\,$ and $\\,b∣c$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $a∣c$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let $k=11.$ Then, $11∣11$ but $33∤11.$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $11∣33.$ Therefore, we have\n$11∣33$ and $33∣k,$ which implies that $11∣k.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $3∣33.$ Therefore, we have\n$3∣33$ and $33∣k,$ which implies that $3∣k.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
